   "claims": [
    "To get the money factor, divide the APR on the lease by 24 or 2400, depending on whether it is expressed as a decimal or percent.",
    "In the U.S., standard auto leases generally allow annual mileage limits of 10,000 to 15,000, with most coming in at 12,000.",
    "Explanation of How the Calculator Computes Monthly Leases Take a car leasable for 3 years and has an agreed-upon value of $50,000 after negotiations on the auto price (capitalized cost) as an example.",
    "The lending financial institution for the lease has placed a residual value of $25,000 on the car after the 3 years and has given the lessee an APR of 6% after a down payment of $8,000.",
    "The lessee is also willing to trade in a used car with a value of $5,000, and the transaction occurs in a state with a 6% tax rate.",
    "$50,000 - $8,000 - $5,000 = $37,000 Subtract the residual value as supplied by the financial institution, $37,000 - $25,000 = $12,000 This is the amount that needs to be amortized over the life of the lease."
   ],
